# Intonare project rules

## Architecture

This is a single-file HTML/CSS/JS app (~131,000+ lines). All code
lives in one IIFE-structured file. There is no build system for the
app itself. Capacitor + Vite handle native wrapping only.

## Editing the file

- Use exact string replacement, not regex. Find a unique string,
  replace it. Verify the match is unique before applying.
- Never use DOTALL regex on this file. It is ~12MB / ~137k lines.
- Never rewrite or re-save the whole file for a small change. No
  `Write` of full `Intonare.html`. No "read all → edit → write all."
- Prefer a short Python patch via `tools/patch_intonare.py` (bytes-safe
  exact `old → new`, uniqueness check, integrity gate after write).
  One-shot scripts under `tools/` are fine if they read/write **bytes**
  the same way; delete them after a successful run.
- **Never** `Path.read_text` / `write_text` / text-mode `open` on
  `Intonare.html` on Windows. That turns CRLF into LF in memory; writing
  back drops ~140 KB and looks like a “successful” edit. Same class of
  failure as truncate.
- Do not put multi-line patches in PowerShell `-c` / inline Python.
  Windows quoting corrupts the patch and can write garbage.
- After any edit, run `node --check` on every script block to catch
  syntax errors. Also run
  `python tools/audits/intonare_html_integrity.py` (or `tools\ship_check.bat`).
- The marketing version lives in two declarations that must stay in
  sync: the HTML comment (`INTONARE_VERSION:`) and the JS const. The
  Settings footer reads the const; do not treat it as a third literal.

## Preflight (idiot-proof) — truncate / CRLF hangup

Real failure modes (hit more than once):
1. An editor/tool reports success but `Intonare.html` silently shrinks
   (e.g. ~12MB → ~8MB). Truncate.
2. A text-mode Python rewrite strips CRLF → LF (~140 KB drop). The file
   still ends with `</html>` and still “works,” then weirdly breaks later.

Before editing `Intonare.html`:
- Record byte size and that the file ends with `</html>`.

After every edit of `Intonare.html` (including ApplyPatch /
  StrReplace / Python patch):
- Re-check byte size. Expect ~12MB. A drop of more than ~80KB for a
  small patch means truncate or newline rewrite; do not keep editing
  that copy.
- Confirm the file still ends with `</html>`.
- Confirm CSS brace count diff is ~0 (`{` vs `}`).
- Tool "success" is not proof. Size + `</html>` + integrity script are
  proof. `tools\ship_check.bat` runs the integrity gate first.

If size collapsed or `</html>` is missing:
1. Stop. Do not re-patch the damaged file.
2. Restore: `git checkout -- Intonare.html` (or the last known good
   commit for that file only).
3. Re-apply with `tools/patch_intonare.py` (or another **bytes-only**
   exact replace).
4. Re-run the integrity script / ship_check.

Prefer Node/shell whole-file writes only for tiny top-of-file
inserts; never treat a truncated copy as success.

## Version / changelog (as you go)

Do not batch version notes until ship day. After each user-facing
`Intonare.html` change (or shipped audit/tool that defines what
shipped):

1. Bump the patch in both places: HTML comment `INTONARE_VERSION:` and
   JS `const INTONARE_VERSION`. They must stay equal.
2. Write a new top `## vX` in `CHANGELOG.md` in the same turn. Cover
   the full batch (chrome, modules, tooling, scope limits if relevant).
3. Codemagic / `ship_check.bat` only verify the match. They are not
   permission to skip the entry during daily work.

Before any Codemagic / TestFlight build:
- Top `CHANGELOG.md` `## vX` must equal `INTONARE_VERSION`.
- HTML comment and JS const must agree.
- Codemagic auto-trigger is OFF; start the build manually.
- Run `tools\ship_check.bat` (sentinel + changelog gate) before calling
  it a ship.

Android deploy:
- Day-to-day: `go.bat`, then Android Studio Run, then clear device cache.
- Play upload day only: `go.bat`, then `release.bat`, then signed AAB.
- Native masters live in `native_src/`. Never "fix" only under `android/`.

## Language

- American English throughout. Exceptions that must not be changed:
  Daft Punk, Licence to Kill, Hapshash and the Coloured Coat,
  `createAnalyser`/`AnalyserNode`, `userCancelled`, Baduizm.
- "Semitone" and "bar" are standard American music usage; do not
  replace with "half step" or "measure."
- No em-dashes in user-facing copy. Use semicolons, commas, or
  periods.

## Audio

- One shared AudioContext via `getAudio()`. Never create a second
  one. iOS Safari caps at ~6 contexts and the Capacitor WebView
  goes silent.
- Use the existing `buildLimiterChain()` pattern for all audio output.
- Parameter changes during playback need the bus-swap pattern: fade
  old buses, create fresh ones, reset the scheduler. Gain muting
  alone is not enough.

## CSS

- CSS brace balance must stay at zero diff. Stray braces silently
  break large sections.
- `@property`-registered custom properties are used for animated
  theme transitions. Don't remove them.
- `transition: all` fights requestAnimationFrame updates. Use
  targeted transitions on non-conflicting properties only.

## Common bugs

- Variable named `t` shadows the global `t()` translation function.
  Name loop vars `tone`, `trk`, `tick`, `mode`, etc.
- Dead code: static analysis misses onclick, querySelector, and IIFE
  invocations. Do a full-file sweep before deleting any function.
- Capacitor: `npx cap sync` alone doesn't reliably update assets.
  The go.bat uses manual copy commands. Clear device cache after
  every Android Studio run.

## Workflow

- Prototype-first for visual or audio changes: build a standalone
  HTML mockup, review on-device, then touch the main file.
- On-device screenshots are the reliable verification method.
- When auditing the app, do a real read of the actual screens. A
  regex count is not a finding.
- Read docs/PROJECT_STATUS.md for current state, pending items, and
  known gotchas. New chats should also load the always-apply rule
  `daniele.mdc` (who Daniele is, voice, workflow).
